 There are two main components to any database system: the database itself and the write-ahead log. The database stores the data, while the log captures all the modifications made to the database, along with the time stamps when they were made. Here’s a simple example of how a write-ahead log works. When we insert a row into the database, the log is updated first. The log is then flushed to the disk, ensuring it is safe from power failures or crashes. This ensures the database is in a consistent state, even if the system crashes.
The database is not updated until the log has been flushed to disk, and this is done in memory. This makes the database recoverable after a crash. When the database system starts up again, it can replay the log, bringing the database back to where it was before the crash. This way, even if the database crashes before the changes are written to disk, the system can recover all the changes.
