The write-ahead log (WAL) is a mechanism used to ensure data consistency in a database even in the event of a crash or power failure. When a database makes a modification to the data, the change is first written to a log, which is essentially a sequential file that stores all changes in order. By writing to the log first, the database ensures that any modifications made are recorded and can be recovered in the event of a failure.

The log is composed of log records, each of which contains a sequence number and a block of data. The sequence number is used to track the order in which log records are written. When a database crashes, it can recover by replaying the log records in the correct order. The database reads the log records from the file, applying each one in sequence, thus restoring the data to a consistent state.

